{"verdict":"incorrect","findings":[{"id":"reviewer#1","title":"Convert legacy estimates from cents before formatting","comment":"`baseRateFor` now returns integer cents, but this caller still passes that value to `roundDollars` as though it were dollars. Every valid legacy estimate is therefore displayed 100× too high—for example, a 2 kg domestic parcel returns `\"590.00\"`, which the marketing widget renders as $590.00 instead of $5.90—so convert the cents value to dollars before calling `toFixed`.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.X19PEl/api.ts","lines":"75","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#2","title":"Keep exact maximum weights in their current tier","comment":"Using `<` here contradicts the documented inclusive `maxKg` boundary and moves parcels weighing exactly 1, 5, or 20 kg into the next, more expensive tier. For example, a 1 kg domestic shipment now has a $4.95 base rate instead of $3.70; restore the inclusive comparison so only weights above a boundary advance tiers.","priority":"P2","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.X19PEl/rates.ts","lines":"42","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#3","title":"Restore positive finite weight validation","comment":"`quoteShipment` now delegates directly to `buildBreakdown` without the previous positive-finite guard, while `parseQuoteRequest` verifies only that the value is a number. Consequently, malformed numeric inputs reach pricing and return successful responses—for example, `{ zone: \"domestic\", weightKg: 0 }` now returns HTTP 200 with a $2.50 quote instead of HTTP 400, and negative or non-finite values can also produce nonsensical totals—so restore validation before building the breakdown and preserve the handler's 400 response.","priority":"P2","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.X19PEl/quote.ts","lines":"82-83","sources":["reviewer"]}],"lenses":[],"target":{"base":"main","baseCommit":"